# question: If a clinician encounters a clinical alarm, which other alarms provide information about the same underlying process?
# rationale: Combines the same two routes as CQ3 to group alarms by the
#   physiological process they relate to, whether diagnostically (via a
#   measurement) or therapeutically (via the device's function).
#
# CORRECTED after a self-audit (see cq_discoveries.md's "audit correction"
# entry) -- the first version only reached PhysiologicalProcess via the
# DIAGNOSTIC chain (Metric -approximates-> Property -isPropertyOf->
# Process). It missed the independent THERAPEUTIC chain inference.ttl also
# declares as a class-level bridge: Device -administers-> TherapeuticModality
# -targetsProcess-> Process, which fires from the device's TYPE alone --
# no Metric required. A bare technical fault on a MechanicalVentilator
# (e.g. "SERVOU - Volume uit blokkade", no Metric at all) still entails
# "this device treats PulmonaryVentilation" purely from its device class.
# Excluding that path undercounted every process a device-administered
# therapy targets: PulmonaryVentilation was reported as 13 alarms, corrected
# to 24; ThermoRegulation 2 -> 9; PulmonaryOxygenation 4 -> 6.
# VascularCompliance (5) and CardiacContraction (3) are genuinely unaffected
# -- no administers-bridged device targets either process, so nothing
# therapeutic reaches them.
#
# One alarm can legitimately land in two different process groups via the
# two different chains at once: "CARDIOHELP - pVen onder waarschuwingsgrens"
# reaches VascularCompliance diagnostically (its VenousPressure metric) AND
# PulmonaryOxygenation therapeutically (the ECMO device it's on administers
# membrane oxygenation) -- both true, not a duplicate/bug.
#
# "Clinical alarm" is still defined functionally, not via mda:hasCategory
# (see the original reasoning below, unchanged by this correction): does
# it resolve to *some* process by *either* chain, not what the annotator
# happened to label it.
#
# reasoning: required
#
# ORIGINAL functional-vs-category reasoning (still holds): checked whether
# alarmcat:Physiological/Technical predicts process-reachability -- no --
# 3 alarmcat:Technical alarms reach a process anyway (MEDIBUS - Air
# temperature high/low -> ThermoRegulation; PHILIPSMONITOR - SpO2 lage PFI
# -> VascularCompliance via TissuePerfusion), while every alarmcat:
# Physiological alarm reaches one. Filtering on hasCategory would wrongly
# exclude those 3 from being valid "clinical alarm" triggers.
#
# Same known-incomplete diagnostic chain as CQ3 (see cq_discoveries.md):
# metric:Pulse never reaches a process via the diagnostic route (no
# approximates bridge at all) and metric:Humidity dead-ends at
# PhysiologicalProperty -- neither is on an administers-bridged device
# either, so both remain silently absent below.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>

SELECT ?process (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?label; separator=" | ") AS ?alarms)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?a) AS ?matchCount) WHERE {
  ?a a mda:AlarmType ; mda:hasLabel ?label ; mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy ?dev .
  {
    ?a mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy/mda:hasFunctionalUnit/mda:hasSensor/mda:sensorProducesSignal/(mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesSignal)*/mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesMetric/mda:approximates/mda:isPropertyOf ?process .
  } UNION {
    ?dev mda:administers/mda:targetsProcess ?process .
  }
}
GROUP BY ?process
HAVING (COUNT(DISTINCT ?a) > 1)
ORDER BY DESC(?matchCount)
